1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,050
Harvard Business Review published this headline.

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:05,550
Most people can't pronounce the key word.

3
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:06,610
The headline:

4
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:10,790
"Leaders Need to Grapple with
Employee Ambivalence on AI."

5
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,670
The word is ambivalence.

6
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:16,150
It means you feel two conflicting

7
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:17,750
things at the same time.

8
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:21,300
Employees are excited about AI
saving them time

9
00:00:21,310 --> 00:00:24,030
and terrified it will replace them.

10
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:27,080
That's ambivalence, not confusion.

11
00:00:27,090 --> 00:00:30,230
Two strong feelings pulling
in opposite directions.

12
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:31,910
Next time a colleague says,

13
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:34,460
"I have mixed feelings about this,"

14
00:00:34,470 --> 00:00:37,110
you can say: "That's ambivalence.

15
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:38,440
It's normal.

16
00:00:38,450 --> 00:00:40,460
Let's talk through both sides."

17
00:00:40,470 --> 00:00:41,880
Ambivalence: am-BIV-uh-lens.

18
00:00:41,890 --> 00:00:43,800
Stress on the second syllable.

19
00:00:43,810 --> 00:00:45,120
Follow and subscribe

20
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:48,550
for more English tips and vocabulary.
